# The weave pass

`WeaveBuilder` is weave's one pass. It reads the whole corpus and returns a `WovenSite` — the book as a page for each chapter and a menu over them all, ready for a presentation to render. It mirrors the product pass. Where `ProductBuilder` composes each document into the code it hides, `WeaveBuilder` projects the corpus into the literate document a reader browses.

The pass reads the parse, not the product. A woven page holds each section's prose and code as the author wrote them, in reading order, and turns every `::[…]` anchor into a link rather than inlining what it names. So the pass keeps what tangle discards, and leaves referenced what tangle would resolve.

## What this module draws on

The pass reads the Loom AST and writes the woven model, so it draws from both. From `effect` come the array and option combinators, `Order` for the reading order the pages come out in, and `pipe` for the spine. `byTypeOr`, from the node foundation, tells a section's runs and markers apart. From the Loom side come the section a page is built from, the preamble and section-body wefts its runs are made of, `boxOf` and `boxKind`, which find a note among them, and the anchor token a code link resolves. The corpus supplies `catalogueOf` and `resolveAnchor`, the same lookup the editor navigates by, so a code anchor reaches the very section a bare `::[…]` composes, with the `Location` that names a loom and the `Position` of an anchor. It supplies `bookOf` and `isBookLoom` too, which tell the book a loom stands in and the loom that opens a book, and the concepts the index lists: `conceptAt`, which finds the concept a section defines, `conceptNames` and `foldFirst`, which tell the words a concept answers to, `definitionLinesOf`, which gives a concept's definition, and `sectionLinksOf`, which gives the links a section makes. The scopes of concepts supply `vocabulariesIn` and `recognisedIn`, which find the plain words each loom recognises, each a `Recognised`. The product pass supplies `sectionNameOf` and the product model `keyOf`, which name a section, and the projection supplies `unsettledRef`, which tells a reference that stops short of code. The contents pass supplies the corpus's structure: `buildContents` reads the corpus's frontmatter into its books, each with its parts and chapters, its orphan chapters, and the back matter that lists its discussions, which the navigation projects into its tree. The same pass supplies the function `titleOf`, which says what a loom is called. From the woven side come the types of everything the pass builds: the corpus, its books, its parts and entries, its pages, the source anchor each block records, and the links, recognised words, and unsettled spans a page holds. A block carries a tag, so the schemas of the blocks come too, and `nodeOf` from the node foundation constructs a block of each.

=>

import { Array, Context, Effect, Layer, Option, Order, pipe } from 'effect'
import type { LoomSection } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import {
  boxOf,
  type PreambleWeft,
  type SectionBodyWeft,
  type TildeWeft,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/Weft'
import { boxKind, noteKind, type WarpAnchorToken } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import {
  bookOf,
  catalogueOf,
  type Catalogue,
  conceptAt,
  conceptNames,
  type ConceptRef,
  definitionLinesOf,
  foldFirst,
  isBookLoom,
  type Location,
  type LoomCorpusAst,
  type LoomModule,
  resolveAnchor,
  sectionLinksOf,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { nodeOf, type Position, byTypeOr } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import { keyOf } from '@athrio/loom-ast/ProductAst'
import { sectionNameOf } from '#ast/ProductBuilder'
import { unsettledRef } from '#ast/LoomVirtualCodeBuilder'
import { type Recognised, recognisedIn, vocabulariesIn } from '#ast/ConceptScope'
import {
  type AnchorLink,
  CodeBlockSchema,
  HeadingBlockSchema,
  MessageBlockSchema,
  NoteBlockSchema,
  ProseBlockSchema,
  type RecognisedWord,
  type SourceAnchor,
  type UnsettledSpan,
  type WovenBlock,
  type WovenBook,
  type WovenCorpus,
  type WovenNavEntry,
  type WovenPage,
  type WovenPart,
} from './WovenCorpus'
import {
  backMatterHeading,
  buildContents,
  orphansHeading,
  titleOf,
  type TocBackMatter,
  type TocBook,
  type TocChapter,
  type TocBackLoom,
  type TocPart,
} from './TableOfContentsBuilder'
import { sectionLanguage } from '../ast/ProductBuilder'

## The builder and its spine

The rest of the pipeline calls the service `WeaveBuilder`. Its one method, `build`, wraps the pure `buildCorpus` in an Effect so callers compose it like any other pass. It may name the loom the weave starts from, the root.

The function `buildCorpus` is the spine. It reads the corpus once for the `Weaving` its pages draw on, and reads the corpus's contents from the root. It projects the default book's contents into the navigation tree, and each named book's into a `WovenBook` with a tree of its own. It maps each loom to its page, adds the index page of each book that defines a concept, and pairs the trees and the pages into a `WovenCorpus`. A page carries the name of the part it sits under, so the spine reads that from the trees it just built.

Every loom yields a page, and so does the root. The root opens the corpus, as `book.loom` opens this book with its title and its preface, and the contents list it under no part. Its page is the page a reader opens the corpus on, as a book loom's page opens its book, and the woven corpus names it as its `opening`, by its title and its slug. A post woven by itself is a corpus of one loom, and its page is that opening page. A weave that names no root, or a root the corpus does not hold, has no opening page.

Then the spine sorts. A corpus is a map, and a map hands back its values in the order they were put in — which is the order the loader finished reading them, not an order anyone chose. Left alone, the pages come out in whatever sequence the filesystem answered that morning, and a loader running alongside other work answers differently from one running by itself. The navigation is already in reading order, so the pages take theirs from it: the root's page first, then the default book's chapters, then each named book's page and chapters. A woven corpus is committed to a repository and read by a site that opens on its first page; it has to come out the same way twice.

=>

export class WeaveBuilder extends Context.Service<WeaveBuilder>()('WeaveBuilder', {
  make: Effect.succeed({
    build: (corpus: LoomCorpusAst, root?: Location): Effect.Effect<WovenCorpus> =>
      Effect.sync(() => buildCorpus(corpus, root)),
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

export const buildCorpus = (corpus: LoomCorpusAst, root?: Location): WovenCorpus => {
  const modules = Array.fromIterable(corpus.modules.values())
  const weaving = weavingOf(corpus.modules)
  const slugOf = (path: Location): string => pageSlug(weaving.catalogue, path)
  const contents = buildContents(corpus, root)
  const uses = usesByConcept(weaving, modules)
  const indexes = Array.getSomes(
    contents.books.map((book) => indexPageOf(weaving, modules, uses, book.name)),
  )
  const indexOf = (book: string): Option.Option<WovenNavEntry> =>
    Option.map(
      Array.findFirst(indexes, (page) => page.slug === indexSlug(book)),
      (page): WovenNavEntry => ({ number: '', title: page.title, slug: page.slug }),
    )
  const nav = Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(
      Array.findFirst(contents.books, (book) => book.name === ''),
      (book) => bookNav(slugOf, book, indexOf(book.name)),
    ),
    (): ReadonlyArray<WovenPart> => [],
  )
  const books = contents.books.filter((book) => book.name !== '').map(wovenBook(slugOf, indexOf))
  const partOfSlug = partIndex([...nav, ...books.flatMap((book) => book.nav)])
  const opening = pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(root),
    Option.flatMap((path) => Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path))),
    Option.map((module): WovenNavEntry =>
      ({ number: '', title: titleOf(module), slug: slugOf(module.path) }),
    ),
  )
  const pages = pipe(
    [...modules.map((module) => buildPage(module, weaving, partOfSlug)), ...indexes],
    Array.sort(
      inReadingOrder(
        readingOrderOf(
          Option.map(opening, (entry) => entry.slug),
          nav,
          books,
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )
  return {
    nav,
    books,
    ...Option.match(opening, { onNone: () => ({}), onSome: (entry) => ({ opening: entry }) }),
    pages,
  }
}

## What a page marks as unsettled

A reference to a section that holds no code yet is to be settled, as the chapter "Names through the book" describes, and the woven page marks it. A `Weaving` carries what every page draws on: the corpus's `Catalogue`, the anchors that stop short of code, and the sections they stop at. It also carries the plain words each loom recognises as concepts, as the chapter "The scope of a concept" describes. `weavingOf` builds it once. It asks the projection's `unsettledRef` of every edge in every module's product, so the page marks exactly the references the tangle refuses, and it reads every loom's recognised words through the corpus's vocabularies. `anchorKey` names an anchor by its loom and the offset of its name.

=>

type Weaving = {
  readonly catalogue: Catalogue
  readonly anchors: ReadonlySet<string>
  readonly sections: ReadonlySet<string>
  readonly recognised: ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyArray<Recognised>>
}

const anchorKey = (path: Location, at: Position): string => `${path}#${at.start.offset}`

const weavingOf = (modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>): Weaving => {
  const catalogue = catalogueOf(modules)
  const vocabularies = vocabulariesIn(modules)
  const unsettled = Array.fromIterable(modules.values()).flatMap((module) =>
    module.product.code.flatMap((code) =>
      Array.getSomes(
        code.fragments.map((part) =>
          part.type === 'NameRef'
            ? unsettledRef(modules, catalogue, module.path, part)
            : Option.none(),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )
  return {
    catalogue,
    anchors: new Set(unsettled.map((one) => anchorKey(one.path, one.anchor))),
    sections: new Set(unsettled.flatMap((one) => Option.toArray(Option.map(one.section, keyOf)))),
    recognised: new Map(
      Array.fromIterable(modules.values()).map((module) => [
        module.path,
        recognisedIn(vocabularies, module),
      ]),
    ),
  }
}

## Slugs and identifiers

A page is addressed by a `slug`, and a link points at a `slug` and a heading `id`. `slugFor` builds a slug from a loom's file path — the last two segments without the `.loom` extension, so `03-the-shape-of-a-loom/06-the-corpus.loom` addresses as `03-the-shape-of-a-loom/06-the-corpus`. `slugify` folds a heading title into the fragment a link targets, lowercasing it and joining its words with dashes. A heading and every anchor that names it slugify the same way, so the link and its target agree.

Two books may lay out their folders alike, and two of their chapters would then share a slug. So `pageSlug` puts a named book's own slug before the slugs of its chapters, as `the-game/01-waking/01-greetings`. The book loom's page takes the book's slug alone, as `the-game`, and a chapter of the default book keeps the slug its path gives.

`sliceContent` reads a block's text straight from the module source between two offsets, dropping only trailing blank space. Reading the source itself, rather than rejoining wefts, keeps a block byte-for-byte as the author wrote it and keeps every offset true, so an anchor's place in the block is its place in the source, shifted by the block's start.

=>

type Span = { readonly start: number; readonly end: number }

const slugFor = (path: string): string =>
  pipe(path.replace(/\.loom$/, '').split('/'), Array.takeRight(2), Array.join('/'))

const slugify = (text: string): string =>
  text
    .toLowerCase()
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, '')

const pageSlug = (catalogue: Catalogue, path: Location): string => {
  const module = Option.fromNullishOr(catalogue.modules.get(path))
  return Option.match(Option.flatMap(module, bookOf), {
    onNone: () => slugFor(path),
    onSome: (book) =>
      Option.exists(module, isBookLoom) ? slugify(book) : `${slugify(book)}/${slugFor(path)}`,
  })
}

const isBlankSource = (source: string): boolean => source.trim().length === 0

const sliceContent = (text: string, span: Span): string =>
  text.slice(span.start, span.end).replace(/\s+$/, '')

## The navigation tree

The navigation is a book's contents, projected into the woven model. `buildContents` has already read the corpus's frontmatter into its books and their ordered parts and chapters, so the weave only turns each into its woven node, with the slug `pageSlug` gives each loom. `wovenPart` maps a part — its number, empty for a numberless one, its name, and its chapters. `wovenEntry` maps a chapter to a `WovenNavEntry`: its number, its title, the slug its path opens, the status and priority a board reads, and its language.

`bookNav` builds one book's tree. Its parts come first. `orphansPart` turns the orphan chapters into one more part, named `Orphan chapters`, which closes the body. `backMatterPart` turns the back matter into a last part, named `Back matter`, and `wovenBackLoom` gives each loom at the back, a discussion or a loom of a kind a trope declares for the back, an entry with its title and the slug its path opens. The book's index closes the back matter, when the book defines a concept. A book with no orphans has no such part, and a book with neither discussions nor concepts has no back matter, so the tree ends on its last part. `wovenBook` gives a named book its `WovenBook`: its name, the slug of its book loom's page, and its tree.

The function `partIndex` inverts the finished trees into a slug-to-part-name lookup, the breadcrumb each page shows. The function `readingOrderOf` lists every slug in reading order and gives each slug its position: the slug of the page the corpus opens on, when the weave names a root, then the default book's tree, then each named book's page and tree. A slug listed twice keeps its first position, so a weave that starts from a book loom opens on that book's page. The order `inReadingOrder` sorts pages by that position and falls back to the slug, so a chapter the navigation does not list still lands somewhere fixed.

=>

type SlugOf = (path: Location) => string

const wovenEntry =
  (slugOf: SlugOf) =>
  (chapter: TocChapter): WovenNavEntry =>
    ({
      number: chapter.number,
      title: chapter.title,
      slug: slugOf(chapter.path),
      status: chapter.status,
      priority: chapter.priority,
      language: chapter.language,
    })

const wovenPart =
  (slugOf: SlugOf) =>
  (part: TocPart): WovenPart => ({
    number: part.number,
    name: part.name,
    chapters: part.chapters.map(wovenEntry(slugOf)),
  })

const wovenBackLoom =
  (slugOf: SlugOf) =>
  (loom: TocBackLoom): WovenNavEntry =>
    ({
      number: '',
      title: loom.title,
      slug: slugOf(loom.path),
    })

const orphansPart = (
  slugOf: SlugOf,
  orphans: ReadonlyArray<TocChapter>,
): ReadonlyArray<WovenPart> =>
  orphans.length === 0
    ? []
    : [
        {
          number: '',
          name: orphansHeading,
          chapters: orphans.map(wovenEntry(slugOf)),
        },
      ]

const backMatterPart = (
  slugOf: SlugOf,
  backMatter: TocBackMatter,
  index: Option.Option<WovenNavEntry>,
): ReadonlyArray<WovenPart> => {
  const entries = [...backMatter.looms.map(wovenBackLoom(slugOf)), ...Option.toArray(index)]
  return entries.length === 0
    ? []
    : [{ number: '', name: backMatterHeading, chapters: entries }]
}

const bookNav = (
  slugOf: SlugOf,
  book: TocBook,
  index: Option.Option<WovenNavEntry>,
): ReadonlyArray<WovenPart> => [
  ...book.parts.map(wovenPart(slugOf)),
  ...orphansPart(slugOf, book.orphans),
  ...backMatterPart(slugOf, book.backMatter, index),
]

const wovenBook =
  (slugOf: SlugOf, indexOf: (book: string) => Option.Option<WovenNavEntry>) =>
  (book: TocBook): WovenBook =>
    ({
      name: book.name,
      slug: book.path === '' ? '' : slugOf(book.path),
      nav: bookNav(slugOf, book, indexOf(book.name)),
    })

const partIndex = (nav: ReadonlyArray<WovenPart>): ReadonlyMap<string, string> =>
  new Map(nav.flatMap((part) => part.chapters.map((chapter) => [chapter.slug, part.name] as const)))

const slugsOf = (nav: ReadonlyArray<WovenPart>): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  nav.flatMap((part) => part.chapters.map((chapter) => chapter.slug))

const readingOrderOf = (
  opening: Option.Option<string>,
  nav: ReadonlyArray<WovenPart>,
  books: ReadonlyArray<WovenBook>,
): ReadonlyMap<string, number> =>
  new Map(
    Array.dedupe([
      ...Option.toArray(opening),
      ...slugsOf(nav),
      ...books.flatMap((book) => [...(book.slug === '' ? [] : [book.slug]), ...slugsOf(book.nav)]),
    ]).map((slug, at) => [slug, at] as const),
  )

const inReadingOrder = (order: ReadonlyMap<string, number>): Order.Order<WovenPage> =>
  Order.combineAll([
    Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (page: WovenPage) =>
      Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(order.get(page.slug)), () => order.size),
    ),
    Order.mapInput(Order.String, (page: WovenPage) => page.slug),
  ])

## A page and its blocks

The function `buildPage` builds a loom's page, and every loom has one. The page takes its slug from `pageSlug` and its title from `titleOf`, which the chapter "The contents of a book" defines: the title the frontmatter names, or the first heading, or the loom's opening words. It takes its part from the navigation tree, which a book loom's page and the root's page lack, its book from the loom's `Book:` line, its frontmatter from the source text the frontmatter node covers, and its blocks from the loom's prose and code.

A loom may open with prose before its first heading, and a short post may hold no heading at all. That prose leads the page, as it leads the loom. The function `leadingBlocks` turns it into blocks the way a section's prose is turned, so a note in it still stands apart and an anchor in it is still a link. These blocks belong to no section, so their `source` anchor names the page alone, with an empty section.

After the leading prose, `buildBlocks` walks the loom's sections in order, and `sectionBlocks` turns one section into its blocks: its heading, then a block for each run of its body. Every block a section yields shares one `source` anchor, which `sectionSource` builds from the chapter's slug and the section's heading id — the pin back to the loom. The function `headingBlock` reads the heading's level from the count of its `#` marks, and a division's as the second, as Markdown reads a line underlined with dashes, and its title straight from the source. It marks the heading `unsettled` when the weaving holds its section among those to be settled, and a `divider` when a divider opened the section. Cutting the body into runs is the next section's work.

=>

const buildPage = (
  module: LoomModule,
  weaving: Weaving,
  partOfSlug: ReadonlyMap<string, string>,
): WovenPage => {
  const slug = pageSlug(weaving.catalogue, module.path)
  return {
    slug,
    title: titleOf(module),
    part: partOfSlug.get(slug),
    book: Option.getOrUndefined(bookOf(module)),
    frontmatter: module.doc.frontmatter?.source,
    blocks: buildBlocks(module, weaving),
  }
}

const leadingBlocks = (module: LoomModule, weaving: Weaving): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> =>
  proseBlocks(module, weaving, module.doc.preamble, {
    chapter: pageSlug(weaving.catalogue, module.path),
    section: '',
  })

const buildBlocks = (module: LoomModule, weaving: Weaving): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> => [
  ...leadingBlocks(module, weaving),
  ...module.doc.sections.flatMap(sectionBlocks(module, weaving)),
]

const sectionSource = (module: LoomModule, weaving: Weaving, section: LoomSection): SourceAnchor =>
  ({
    chapter: pageSlug(weaving.catalogue, module.path),
    section: slugify(titleSource(section)),
  })

const sectionBlocks =
  (module: LoomModule, weaving: Weaving) =>
  (section: LoomSection): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> => {
    const source = sectionSource(module, weaving, section)
    const runs = splitRuns(sectionBody(section))
    return [
      headingBlock(module, weaving, section, source),
      ...runs.flatMap((run, index) =>
        runBlocks(module, weaving, section, source)(run, tildeOpens(Array.get(runs, index + 1))),
      ),
    ]
  }

const titleSource = (section: LoomSection): string => section.heading.title?.source ?? ''

const levelOf = byTypeOr<LoomSection['heading']['marker']>()<number>(
  { Divider: () => 2 },
  (marker) => Math.max(1, marker.source.replace(/[^#]/g, '').length),
)

const headingLevel = (section: LoomSection): number => levelOf(section.heading.marker)

const headingBlock = (
  module: LoomModule,
  weaving: Weaving,
  section: LoomSection,
  source: SourceAnchor,
): WovenBlock =>
  nodeOf(HeadingBlockSchema, {
    source,
    level: headingLevel(section),
    title: titleSource(section),
    id: slugify(titleSource(section)),
    unsettled: weaving.sections.has(keyOf({ path: module.path, name: sectionNameOf(section) })),
    divider: section.heading.marker.type === 'Divider',
  })

## Splitting a section into runs

A section's body is its preamble and its code together. `sectionBody` joins the two, so a quote that opens in the leading prose and runs on stays one prose block, not two split at the seam. `splitRuns` cuts that body into runs: a run is one unbroken stretch of one mode, and a run that opens with no marker is prose — the leading paragraphs, the prose after fenced code, or a quote the classifier left verbatim. `startsRun` says where a cut falls: at each `=>` and `~`, and on the line after a fence that closes fenced code, since the prose that follows it needs no tilde.

`runBlocks` turns a run into its blocks, and stamps each with the section's `source` anchor. A run that an arrow opened is one code block, holding its verbatim text as `code`; a prose run is its prose, with each note split out into a note block by `proseBlocks`, so a reader's annotation stands apart from the text around it. `proseBlockOf` keeps the prose as Markdown, with a link for each anchor in it that resolves, placed by its offset in the trimmed Markdown. It carries the words the loom recognises within the prose too, each placed the same way by `recognisedWithin`, and carries none when there are none. `runSpan` bounds the content from the first non-blank line to the last, keeping a marker's own inline text but dropping the arrow or tilde itself, so the `code` holds nothing that is not code. A fence line around fenced code bounds no content either, so `pieceOfWeft` counts it as blank, and the span runs from the first line of code to the last.

A note is a box of the `Note` kind, and the classifier has already marked the lines of colons that open and close it. `stretchesOf` cuts a prose run at those marks into stretches, each either a note or the prose between notes. A stretch that a note opens runs to the line that closes it, and `bodyOf` drops the two lines of colons, so a note block holds its body alone. The note's name, when it has one, becomes the block's `caption`, which `captionOfNote` reads from the line that opens the note. A note quoted inside a fence is text, not a note: the classifier marks no line inside a quote, so nothing there can start a stretch of its own. A box of any other kind stays in the prose around it, as the author wrote it.

A tilde means something else in a discussion. In a chapter a tilde only returns to prose, and the text written after it is prose too. In a discussion a tilde opens a message, and the text after it is the name of the message's author. So in a discussion `runBlocks` turns a run that a tilde opens into a message block, then the blocks of the message's words. `messageBlock` reads the author's name from the tilde.

The frame is not lost, though, because a reader learning Loom needs to see it. A code block keeps the arrow that opened it, as it was written. Whether a tilde closed it is a fact about the next run, not this one: `tildeOpens` looks at the run that follows, and a run that begins with a tilde means this block was closed by it. A block followed by nothing, or by the next heading, ran on to its end. `markerInline` reads the code or prose written on an arrow or tilde line; `pieceOfWeft` reads a plain line. `codeLanguage` names a code block's language for the highlighter by the product pass's own rule, `sectionLanguage`: the section's specifier, else the extension of the file its sink names, else the chapter's language. A `{Tangle}` section takes the extension of the file it writes, never the mark itself. So a section that writes `extension.toml` in a chapter of Rust is labelled TOML, as the tangler composes it. `blocksOpenedBy` reads a run by its first line: an arrow opens a block of code, and any other line opens prose.

=>

type BodyWeft = PreambleWeft | SectionBodyWeft

type Piece = {
  readonly start: number
  readonly end: number
  readonly blank: boolean
}

const sectionBody = (section: LoomSection): ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft> => [
  ...section.preamble,
  ...section.code,
]

const isMarker = (weft: BodyWeft): boolean => weft.type === 'ArrowWeft' || weft.type === 'TildeWeft'

const closesFenced = (weft: BodyWeft): boolean => weft.type === 'FenceWeft' && weft.side === 'close'

const startsRun = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>, at: number): boolean =>
  Option.exists(Array.get(wefts, at), isMarker) ||
  Option.exists(Array.get(wefts, at - 1), closesFenced)

const splitRuns = (body: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>> =>
  Array.chop(body, (wefts) => {
    const end = pipe(
      Array.findFirstIndex(Array.drop(wefts, 1), (_, at) => startsRun(wefts, at + 1)),
      Option.map((at) => at + 1),
      Option.getOrElse(() => wefts.length),
    )
    return [Array.take(wefts, end), Array.drop(wefts, end)]
  })

const tildeOpens = (run: Option.Option<ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>>): boolean =>
  pipe(
    run,
    Option.flatMap(Array.head),
    Option.exists((weft) => weft.type === 'TildeWeft'),
  )

const markerInline: (marker: BodyWeft) => Option.Option<Piece> = byTypeOr<BodyWeft>()(
  {
    ArrowWeft: (a) =>
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(a.code), (code) => ({
        start: code.position.start.offset,
        end: a.position.end.offset,
        blank: false,
      })),
    TildeWeft: (t) =>
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(t.prose), (prose) => ({
        start: prose.position.start.offset,
        end: t.position.end.offset,
        blank: false,
      })),
  },
  () => Option.none<Piece>(),
)

const pieceOfWeft = (weft: BodyWeft): Piece => ({
  start: weft.position.start.offset,
  end: weft.position.end.offset,
  blank: weft.type === 'FenceWeft' || isBlankSource(weft.source),
})

const runSpan = (run: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>): Option.Option<Span> => {
  const pieces = run.flatMap((weft, index) =>
    index === 0 && isMarker(weft) ? Option.toArray(markerInline(weft)) : [pieceOfWeft(weft)],
  )
  const kept = pieces.filter((piece) => !piece.blank)
  return Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(kept)
    ? Option.some({
        start: Array.headNonEmpty(kept).start,
        end: Array.lastNonEmpty(kept).end,
      })
    : Option.none()
}

const codeLanguage = (module: LoomModule, section: LoomSection): string => {
  const frontmatter = Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter)
  return sectionLanguage(
    section,
    pipe(
      frontmatter,
      Option.flatMapNullishOr((f) => f.language),
      Option.map((language) => language.value.trim().toLowerCase()),
      Option.getOrElse(() => 'plaintext'),
    ),
    pipe(
      frontmatter,
      Option.flatMapNullishOr((f) => f.package),
      Option.map((pkg) => pkg.value),
    ),
  )
}

type Stretch = { readonly note: boolean; readonly wefts: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft> }

const opensNote = (weft: BodyWeft): boolean =>
  Option.exists(boxOf(weft), (box) => box.side === 'open' && boxKind(box) === noteKind)

const closesBox = (weft: BodyWeft): boolean =>
  Option.exists(boxOf(weft), (box) => box.side === 'close')

const stretchEnd = (wefts: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>, note: boolean): number =>
  note
    ? pipe(
        Array.findFirstIndex(wefts, (weft, at) => at > 0 && closesBox(weft)),
        Option.map((at) => at + 1),
        Option.getOrElse(() => wefts.length),
      )
    : pipe(
        Array.findFirstIndex(wefts, (weft, at) => at > 0 && opensNote(weft)),
        Option.getOrElse(() => wefts.length),
      )

const stretchesOf = (run: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>): ReadonlyArray<Stretch> =>
  Array.chop(run, (wefts) => {
    const note = opensNote(Array.headNonEmpty(wefts))
    const end = stretchEnd(wefts, note)
    return [{ note, wefts: Array.take(wefts, end) }, Array.drop(wefts, end)]
  })

const captionOfNote = (note: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Array.head(note),
    Option.flatMap(boxOf),
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((box) => box.name),
    Option.map((name) => name.value),
  )

const bodyOf = (note: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>): ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft> => {
  const inside = Array.drop(note, 1)
  return Option.exists(Array.last(inside), closesBox) ? Array.dropRight(inside, 1) : inside
}

const textOf = (module: LoomModule, wefts: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>): string =>
  Option.match(runSpan(wefts), {
    onNone: () => '',
    onSome: (span) => sliceContent(module.text, span).trim(),
  })

const proseBlockOf = (
  module: LoomModule,
  weaving: Weaving,
  wefts: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>,
  source: SourceAnchor,
): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> =>
  Option.match(runSpan(wefts), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> => [],
    onSome: (span) => {
      const raw = sliceContent(module.text, span)
      const markdown = raw.trim()
      const start = span.start + raw.length - raw.trimStart().length
      const recognised = recognisedWithin(module, weaving, span, start)
      return markdown.length === 0
        ? []
        : [
            nodeOf(ProseBlockSchema, {
              source,
              markdown,
              links: Array.getSomes(
                anchorsWithin(wefts, span).map((anchor) =>
                  resolveLink(module, weaving, start, anchor),
                ),
              ),
              ...(recognised.length === 0 ? {} : { recognised }),
            }),
          ]
    },
  })

const recognisedWithin = (
  module: LoomModule,
  weaving: Weaving,
  span: Span,
  blockStart: number,
): ReadonlyArray<RecognisedWord> =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.fromNullishOr(weaving.recognised.get(module.path)),
    (): ReadonlyArray<Recognised> => [],
  )
    .filter(
      ({ word }) =>
        word.position.start.offset >= span.start && word.position.end.offset <= span.end,
    )
    .map(({ word, concept }): RecognisedWord => ({
      offset: word.position.start.offset - blockStart,
      length: word.position.end.offset - word.position.start.offset,
      concept: concept.name.source,
      definition: concept.definition,
    }))

const proseBlocks = (
  module: LoomModule,
  weaving: Weaving,
  run: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>,
  source: SourceAnchor,
): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> =>
  stretchesOf(run).flatMap((stretch) =>
    stretch.note
      ? [
          nodeOf(NoteBlockSchema, {
            source,
            caption: Option.getOrUndefined(captionOfNote(stretch.wefts)),
            markdown: textOf(module, bodyOf(stretch.wefts)),
          }),
        ]
      : proseBlockOf(module, weaving, stretch.wefts, source),
  )

const codeBlock = (
  module: LoomModule,
  weaving: Weaving,
  section: LoomSection,
  source: SourceAnchor,
  run: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>,
  span: Span,
  arrow: string,
  closed: boolean,
): WovenBlock =>
  nodeOf(CodeBlockSchema, {
    source,
    language: codeLanguage(module, section),
    code: sliceContent(module.text, span),
    arrow,
    closed,
    links: Array.getSomes(
      anchorsWithin(run, span).map((anchor) => resolveLink(module, weaving, span.start, anchor)),
    ),
    unsettled: anchorsWithin(run, span)
      .filter((anchor) => weaving.anchors.has(anchorKey(module.path, anchor.name.position)))
      .map((anchor): UnsettledSpan => ({
        offset: anchor.position.start.offset - span.start,
        length: anchor.position.end.offset - anchor.position.start.offset,
      })),
  })

const anchorsWithin = (run: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>, span: Span): ReadonlyArray<WarpAnchorToken> =>
  run
    .flatMap((weft) => weft.anchors)
    .filter(
      (anchor) =>
        anchor.position.start.offset >= span.start && anchor.position.end.offset <= span.end,
    )

const isDiscussion = (module: LoomModule): boolean =>
  module.doc.frontmatter?.discussion !== undefined

const messageBlock = (tilde: TildeWeft, source: SourceAnchor): WovenBlock =>
  nodeOf(MessageBlockSchema, {
    source,
    author: Option.getOrUndefined(
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(tilde.prose), (name) => name.source.trim()),
    ),
  })

const blocksOpenedBy = byTypeOr<
  BodyWeft,
  [
    within: {
      readonly module: LoomModule
      readonly weaving: Weaving
      readonly section: LoomSection
      readonly source: SourceAnchor
      readonly run: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>
      readonly span: Span
      readonly closed: boolean
    },
  ]
>()<ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock>>(
  {
    ArrowWeft: (opened, { module, weaving, section, source, run, span, closed }) => [
      codeBlock(
        module,
        weaving,
        section,
        source,
        run,
        span,
        opened.arrow.source.trim(),
        closed,
      ),
    ],
  },
  (_first, { module, weaving, source, run }) =>
    proseBlocks(module, weaving, run, source),
)

const runBlocks =
  (module: LoomModule, weaving: Weaving, section: LoomSection, source: SourceAnchor) =>
  (run: ReadonlyArray<BodyWeft>, closed: boolean): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> =>
    Array.matchLeft(run, {
      onEmpty: (): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> => [],
      onNonEmpty: (first, rest) =>
        first.type === 'TildeWeft' && isDiscussion(module)
          ? [messageBlock(first, source), ...proseBlocks(module, weaving, rest, source)]
          : Option.match(runSpan(run), {
              onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> => [],
              onSome: (span) =>
                blocksOpenedBy(first, { module, weaving, section, source, run, span, closed }),
            }),
    })

## Resolving a code anchor

`resolveLink` turns one `::[…]` anchor into a link. It resolves the anchor the way the composition does, through the corpus's `Catalogue`, and reads the section it reaches: a section of the anchor's own loom, a chapter's opening section, or a section a path names. A link in prose may reach a concept by any of its forms, and then it carries the concept's definition as its title, which `conceptAt` finds. A link to a concept may also write a path that ends in the word its sentence needs, so it shows that word alone, which `wordOf` reads, and not the whole path. The link carries the name the author wrote, the page and heading that section becomes, and the anchor's place within the block, so the presentation makes just those characters a link. An anchor that resolves to nothing yields no link, and stands in the code as the author's own text.

=>

const wordOf = (name: string, concept: ConceptRef): string => {
  const last = name.slice(name.lastIndexOf(',') + 1).trim()
  return conceptNames(concept).some((form) => foldFirst(form) === foldFirst(last)) ? last : name
}

const resolveLink = (
  module: LoomModule,
  weaving: Weaving,
  blockStart: number,
  anchor: WarpAnchorToken,
): Option.Option<AnchorLink> =>
  pipe(
    resolveAnchor(weaving.catalogue, module.path, anchor),
    Option.flatMap((ref) =>
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(ref.section.heading.title), (title): AnchorLink => {
        const concept = conceptAt(weaving.catalogue, ref)
        return {
          name: Option.match(concept, {
            onNone: () => anchor.name.value,
            onSome: (found) => wordOf(anchor.name.value, found),
          }),
          targetSlug: pageSlug(weaving.catalogue, ref.module),
          targetId: slugify(title.source),
          offset: anchor.position.start.offset - blockStart,
          length: anchor.position.end.offset - anchor.position.start.offset,
          title: Option.getOrUndefined(
            pipe(
              concept,
              Option.map((found) => found.definition),
              Option.filter((definition) => definition !== ''),
            ),
          ),
        }
      }),
    ),
  )

## The index at the back

A book that defines concepts closes on its index, the page a reader turns to for a word's meaning, as the chapter "Concepts" describes. Nobody writes it: the weave builds it from the concepts the book's chapters define. `indexPageOf` gives the index page of one book, titled `Index`, which `indexHeading` holds, and nothing for a book with no concepts. Its slug is `index`, after the book's own slug for a named book, which `indexSlug` writes. It lists the book's concepts by name, which `byConceptName` orders, and `bookNameOf` reads the book a concept's loom stands in.

The function `entryBlocks` writes one concept's entry: its name as a heading, its definition with the links it makes, its other forms, and a line that says where the book defines it and where the book uses it. The definition's place is the loom that defines the concept, by its title, linked to the concept's section on that loom's page.

A `Use` is one place the book uses a concept: a loom, and the section the use stands in. A use in the prose that leads a loom stands in no section, so its `section` is empty. The function `usesByConcept` finds the uses of every concept in one pass over the corpus's links and recognised words, keyed by `conceptKey`. It reads each loom as its passages, which the function `passagesOf` lists: the leading prose, then each section. A passage with a link that reaches the concept, or a word that recognises it, is a use, and `conceptsRecognisedIn` finds the concepts a passage's words recognise. The uses are ordered by their page's slug and then by where they stand, as `byUse` orders them. An application that lists a workspace's concepts reads the same uses through `conceptUsesOf`, which builds the weave's view of the corpus and finds the uses of every concept in it, so the index page and the application never disagree. The function `placeOf` writes a use as the title of its section, linked to that section, and writes a use in the leading prose as the title of its loom, linked to the top of the loom's page. The function `linkedBlock` writes the whole line as a prose block from its phrases, each a `Phrase`, with a link on each name it gives, so a reader reaches the definition and each use in one step.

=>

export const indexHeading = 'Index'

const indexSlug = (book: string): string => (book === '' ? 'index' : `${slugify(book)}/index`)

const bookNameOf = (weaving: Weaving, path: Location): string =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.flatMap(Option.fromNullishOr(weaving.catalogue.modules.get(path)), bookOf),
    () => '',
  )

const byConceptName: Order.Order<ConceptRef> = Order.mapInput(Order.String, (concept: ConceptRef) =>
  concept.name.source.toLowerCase(),
)

type Phrase = {
  readonly text: string
  readonly target: Option.Option<{ readonly slug: string; readonly id: string }>
}

const linkedBlock = (source: SourceAnchor, pieces: ReadonlyArray<Phrase>): WovenBlock =>
  nodeOf(ProseBlockSchema, {
    source,
    markdown: pieces.map((piece) => piece.text).join(''),
    links: Array.getSomes(
      Array.mapAccum(pieces, 0, (at, piece) => [
        at + piece.text.length,
        Option.map(piece.target, (target): AnchorLink =>
          ({
            name: piece.text,
            targetSlug: target.slug,
            targetId: target.id,
            offset: at,
            length: piece.text.length,
          }),
        ),
      ])[1],
    ),
  })

export type Use = { readonly module: LoomModule; readonly section: Option.Option<LoomSection> }

export const conceptKey = (module: Location, section: LoomSection): string =>
  `${module}#${section.position.start.offset}`

const startOf = (use: Use): number =>
  Option.match(use.section, {
    onNone: () => 0,
    onSome: (section) => section.position.start.offset,
  })

const byUse = (weaving: Weaving): Order.Order<Use> =>
  Order.combineAll([
    Order.mapInput(Order.String, (use: Use) => pageSlug(weaving.catalogue, use.module.path)),
    Order.mapInput(Order.Number, startOf),
  ])

type Passage = {
  readonly section: Option.Option<LoomSection>
  readonly links: ReadonlyArray<WarpAnchorToken>
  readonly span: Span
}

const passagesOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Passage> => [
  {
    section: Option.none(),
    links: module.doc.preamble.flatMap((line) => line.anchors),
    span: {
      start: 0,
      end: Option.match(Array.head(module.doc.sections), {
        onNone: () => module.text.length,
        onSome: (first) => first.position.start.offset,
      }),
    },
  },
  ...module.doc.sections.map((section): Passage => ({
    section: Option.some(section),
    links: sectionLinksOf(section),
    span: { start: section.position.start.offset, end: section.position.end.offset },
  })),
]

const conceptsRecognisedIn = (
  weaving: Weaving,
  module: LoomModule,
  span: Span,
): ReadonlyArray<ConceptRef> =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.fromNullishOr(weaving.recognised.get(module.path)),
    (): ReadonlyArray<Recognised> => [],
  )
    .filter(
      ({ word }) =>
        word.position.start.offset >= span.start && word.position.end.offset <= span.end,
    )
    .map(({ concept }) => concept)

const usesByConcept = (
  weaving: Weaving,
  modules: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>,
): ReadonlyMap<string, ReadonlyArray<Use>> => {
  const pairs = Array.sort(
    modules.flatMap((module) =>
      passagesOf(module).flatMap((passage) =>
        pipe(
          [
            ...passage.links.flatMap((link) =>
              Option.toArray(
                Option.flatMap(resolveAnchor(weaving.catalogue, module.path, link), (ref) =>
                  conceptAt(weaving.catalogue, ref),
                ),
              ),
            ),
            ...conceptsRecognisedIn(weaving, module, passage.span),
          ].map((concept) => conceptKey(concept.module, concept.section)),
          Array.dedupe,
          Array.map((key) => ({ key, use: { module, section: passage.section } })),
        ),
      ),
    ),
    Order.mapInput(byUse(weaving), (pair: { readonly use: Use }) => pair.use),
  )
  return new Map(
    Object.entries(Array.groupBy(pairs, (pair) => pair.key)).map(
      ([key, found]) => [key, found.map((pair) => pair.use)] as const,
    ),
  )
}

export const conceptUsesOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
): ReadonlyMap<string, ReadonlyArray<Use>> =>
  usesByConcept(weavingOf(modules), Array.fromIterable(modules.values()))

const placeOf = (weaving: Weaving, use: Use): Phrase => {
  const slug = pageSlug(weaving.catalogue, use.module.path)
  return Option.match(use.section, {
    onNone: (): Phrase => ({ text: titleOf(use.module), target: Option.some({ slug, id: '' }) }),
    onSome: (section): Phrase => ({
      text: titleSource(section),
      target: Option.some({ slug, id: slugify(titleSource(section)) }),
    }),
  })
}

const entryBlocks = (
  weaving: Weaving,
  modules: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>,
  usesOf: ReadonlyMap<string, ReadonlyArray<Use>>,
  slug: string,
  concept: ConceptRef,
): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> => {
  const id = slugify(concept.name.source)
  const source: SourceAnchor = { chapter: slug, section: id }
  const forms = concept.forms.map((form) => form.value)
  const home = Array.findFirst(modules, (module) => module.path === concept.module)
  const uses = Option.getOrElse(
    Option.fromNullishOr(usesOf.get(conceptKey(concept.module, concept.section))),
    (): ReadonlyArray<Use> => [],
  )
  return [
    nodeOf(HeadingBlockSchema, {
      source,
      level: 2,
      title: concept.name.source,
      id,
      unsettled: false,
      divider: false,
    }),
    ...Option.match(home, {
      onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<WovenBlock> => [],
      onSome: (module) => proseBlockOf(module, weaving, definitionLinesOf(concept.section), source),
    }),
    ...(forms.length === 0
      ? []
      : [
          nodeOf(ProseBlockSchema, {
            source,
            markdown: `Also: ${forms.join(', ')}.`,
            links: [],
          }),
        ]),
    linkedBlock(source, [
      { text: 'Defined in ', target: Option.none() },
      ...Option.match(home, {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Phrase> => [{ text: concept.name.source, target: Option.none() }],
        onSome: (module): ReadonlyArray<Phrase> => [
          {
            text: titleOf(module),
            target: Option.some({ slug: pageSlug(weaving.catalogue, module.path), id }),
          },
        ],
      }),
      { text: '.', target: Option.none() },
      ...(uses.length === 0
        ? []
        : [
            { text: ' Used in ', target: Option.none() },
            ...uses.flatMap((use, at): ReadonlyArray<Phrase> => [
              ...(at === 0 ? [] : [{ text: ', ', target: Option.none() }]),
              placeOf(weaving, use),
            ]),
            { text: '.', target: Option.none() },
          ]),
    ]),
  ]
}

const indexPageOf = (
  weaving: Weaving,
  modules: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>,
  usesOf: ReadonlyMap<string, ReadonlyArray<Use>>,
  book: string,
): Option.Option<WovenPage> => {
  const slug = indexSlug(book)
  return pipe(
    weaving.catalogue.concepts.filter((concept) => bookNameOf(weaving, concept.module) === book),
    Array.sort(byConceptName),
    Option.liftPredicate(Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty),
    Option.map((concepts): WovenPage => ({
      slug,
      title: indexHeading,
      part: backMatterHeading,
      book: book === '' ? undefined : book,
      blocks: [
        nodeOf(HeadingBlockSchema, {
          source: { chapter: slug, section: 'index' },
          level: 1,
          title: indexHeading,
          id: 'index',
          unsettled: false,
          divider: false,
        }),
        ...concepts.flatMap((concept) => entryBlocks(weaving, modules, usesOf, slug, concept)),
      ],
    })),
  )
}

## Testing what opens a page

The tests below weave small corpora and read the pages that come out. A loom written inside this loom's code would be read as part of it, so a fixture is a list of its lines, each one quoted, and `lines` joins them into the text a file holds. The helper `anchor` spells an anchor in two pieces for the same reason.

The helper `woven` builds a corpus from looms given by path and weaves it, from a root when the case names one. A case reads a page's blocks through `outline`, which gives each block's kind beside its text: a heading's title, a note's body, a block of prose or of code as written.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import { Array, Effect, Layer, Option } from 'effect'
import { byType } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import { LoomCorpusAstBuilder } from '#ast/LoomCorpusAstBuilder'
import { WeaveBuilder } from '../src/weave/WeaveBuilder'
import type { WovenBlock, WovenCorpus, WovenPage } from '../src/weave/WovenCorpus'

const lines = (...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => `${rows.join('\n')}\n`

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

const woven = (looms: Readonly<Record<string, string>>, root?: string): WovenCorpus =>
  Effect.runSync(
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const builder = yield* LoomCorpusAstBuilder
      const modules = yield* Effect.forEach(Object.keys(looms), (path) =>
        builder
          .build(
            { read: (asked) => Effect.succeed(looms[asked] ?? ''), list: Option.none() },
            path,
          )
          .pipe(Effect.map((module) => [path, module] as const)),
      )
      const weaver = yield* WeaveBuilder
      return yield* weaver.build({ modules: new Map(modules) }, root)
    }).pipe(Effect.provide(Layer.mergeAll(LoomCorpusAstBuilder.layer, WeaveBuilder.layer))),
  )

const textOf = byType<WovenBlock>()({
  prose: (block) => block.markdown,
  heading: (block) => block.title,
  code: (block) => block.code,
  note: (block) => block.markdown,
  message: (block) => block.author ?? '',
})

const outline = (page: WovenPage | undefined): ReadonlyArray<readonly [string, string]> =>
  (page?.blocks ?? []).map((block) => [block.type, textOf(block)] as const)

const pageAt = (corpus: WovenCorpus, slug: string): WovenPage | undefined =>
  corpus.pages.find((page) => page.slug === slug)

## Leading prose on the page

A loom may open with prose before its first heading. The first case weaves such a loom and finds that prose on the page, ahead of the heading. The second reads where the block says it came from: the page, and no section of it. The third leads a loom with a note and with a sentence that holds an anchor, and finds the note as a block of its own and the anchor as a link to the section it names.

=>

describe('the prose that leads a loom', () => {
  const post = lines(
    '---',
    'Chapter: A post with a title',
    '---',
    '',
    'Prose before any heading.',
    '',
    '# A post with a title',
    '',
    'Prose under the title.',
  )

  it('stands on the page before the first heading', () => {
    const page = pageAt(woven({ '/posts/titled.loom': post }), 'posts/titled')
    expect(outline(page)).toEqual([
      ['prose', 'Prose before any heading.'],
      ['heading', 'A post with a title'],
      ['prose', 'Prose under the title.'],
    ])
  })

  it('belongs to the page and to no section of it', () => {
    const page = pageAt(woven({ '/posts/titled.loom': post }), 'posts/titled')
    expect(page?.blocks[0]?.source).toEqual({ chapter: 'posts/titled', section: '' })
  })

  it('keeps a note apart and an anchor as a link', () => {
    const led = lines(
      ':::',
      'A note that leads.',
      ':::',
      '',
      `See ${anchor('The details')} first.`,
      '',
      '# A post',
      '',
      '## The details',
      '',
      'They are few.',
    )
    const page = pageAt(woven({ '/posts/led.loom': led }), 'posts/led')
    expect(outline(page).slice(0, 2)).toEqual([
      ['note', 'A note that leads.'],
      ['prose', `See ${anchor('The details')} first.`],
    ])
    const prose = page?.blocks[1]
    expect(prose?.type === 'prose' ? prose.links.map((link) => link.targetId) : []).toEqual([
      'the-details',
    ])
  })
})

## A loom with no heading

A short post may hold no heading at all. Its page still holds all of its prose. The first case gives such a post a title in its frontmatter. The cases after it give it none, and each reads what the loom is then called.

A loom with no title goes by its opening words: its first sentence, without the closing full stop. A question mark stays, since it is part of what the sentence says. A paragraph broken over two lines is read as one. An anchor in the sentence reads as the name it holds. A sentence longer than sixty characters keeps the words that fit and ends on an ellipsis. A box that opens the post is a remark beside its words, so the opening words are read after it. A loom with no prose at all goes by the name of its file.

The last case weaves an untitled post beside a book's opening loom. The menu lists the post among the orphan chapters, under the same words its page is called by.

=>

describe('a loom with no heading', () => {
  const titleOf = (text: string): string | undefined =>
    pageAt(woven({ '/posts/2026-11-21.loom': text }), 'posts/2026-11-21')?.title

  it('weaves to a page that holds all its prose', () => {
    const post = lines(
      '---',
      'Chapter: A short post',
      '---',
      '',
      'Its first line.',
      '',
      'Its second.',
    )
    const page = pageAt(woven({ '/posts/short.loom': post }), 'posts/short')
    expect(page?.title).toBe('A short post')
    expect(outline(page)).toEqual([['prose', 'Its first line.\n\nIts second.']])
  })

  it('goes by its first sentence, without the full stop', () => {
    expect(titleOf(lines('A short post with no heading. Its second sentence.'))).toBe(
      'A short post with no heading',
    )
  })

  it('keeps a question mark', () => {
    expect(titleOf(lines('Why weave at all? Because prose is read.'))).toBe('Why weave at all?')
  })

  it('reads a paragraph broken over two lines as one', () => {
    expect(titleOf(lines('A paragraph broken', 'over two lines. And more.'))).toBe(
      'A paragraph broken over two lines',
    )
  })

  it('reads an anchor as the name it holds', () => {
    expect(titleOf(lines(`Read ${anchor('The details')} first.`))).toBe('Read The details first')
  })

  it('reads its opening words after a box that opens it', () => {
    expect(titleOf(lines(':::', 'Check the date.', ':::', '', 'Spring came early.'))).toBe(
      'Spring came early',
    )
  })

  it('cuts a long sentence at a word and ends it on an ellipsis', () => {
    expect(
      titleOf(
        lines(
          'This opening sentence runs on for far longer than any menu could ever hold in one line.',
        ),
      ),
    ).toBe('This opening sentence runs on for far longer than any menu…')
  })

  it('goes by the name of its file when it holds no prose', () => {
    expect(titleOf('')).toBe('2026-11-21')
  })

  it('stands in the menu under the same words', () => {
    const corpus = woven(
      {
        '/corpus/book.loom': lines('# A small book', '', 'Its preface.'),
        '/corpus/post.loom': lines('A short post with no heading.'),
      },
      '/corpus/book.loom',
    )
    expect(corpus.nav).toEqual([
      {
        number: '',
        name: 'Orphan chapters',
        chapters: [{ number: '', title: 'A short post with no heading', slug: 'corpus/post' }],
      },
    ])
  })
})

## The page a corpus opens on

The loom a weave starts from has a page too, and that page comes first, whatever its slug. The first case weaves a book's opening loom beside a chapter whose slug sorts ahead of it. It finds the opening loom's page first, with its title and its preface, named as the corpus's opening and absent from the menu. The second case weaves a post by itself: a corpus of one loom, whose one page is the post. The third weaves the same book from no root, and the corpus then names no opening page.

=>

describe('the loom a weave starts from', () => {
  it('opens the corpus with a page of its own, ahead of every chapter', () => {
    const corpus = woven(
      {
        '/corpus/zz-book.loom': lines('# A small book', '', 'The preface of the book.'),
        '/corpus/aa-first.loom': lines('---', 'Chapter 1: The first chapter', '---', '', '# First'),
      },
      '/corpus/zz-book.loom',
    )
    expect(corpus.pages.map((page) => page.slug)).toEqual(['corpus/zz-book', 'corpus/aa-first'])
    expect(corpus.opening).toEqual({ number: '', title: 'A small book', slug: 'corpus/zz-book' })
    expect(outline(corpus.pages[0])).toEqual([
      ['heading', 'A small book'],
      ['prose', 'The preface of the book.'],
    ])
    expect(corpus.nav.flatMap((part) => part.chapters.map((chapter) => chapter.slug))).toEqual([
      'corpus/aa-first',
    ])
  })

  it('is the whole of a post woven by itself', () => {
    const corpus = woven({ '/posts/post.loom': lines('Just prose.') }, '/posts/post.loom')
    expect(corpus.nav).toEqual([])
    expect(corpus.opening).toEqual({ number: '', title: 'Just prose', slug: 'posts/post' })
    expect(corpus.pages.map((page) => page.title)).toEqual(['Just prose'])
    expect(outline(corpus.pages[0])).toEqual([['prose', 'Just prose.']])
  })

  it('names no opening page when the weave names no root', () => {
    const corpus = woven({ '/corpus/book.loom': lines('# A small book', '', 'Its preface.') })
    expect(corpus.opening).toBeUndefined()
    expect(corpus.pages.map((page) => page.slug)).toEqual(['corpus/book'])
  })
})

## The index and the opening page

The index says where the book defines a concept and where the book uses it. Both places may now be an opening: a concept may be defined by the loom the weave starts from, and it may be used in prose that leads a loom. The case weaves such a corpus and reads the line that closes the concept's entry. The definition is linked to the concept's section on the opening loom's page. The use is written as the title of the loom whose leading prose holds it, here its opening words, and linked to the top of that loom's page.

=>

describe('the index of a book that opens with prose', () => {
  it('links the opening loom that defines a concept, and a use in leading prose', () => {
    const corpus = woven(
      {
        '/corpus/book.loom': lines(
          '# A small book',
          '',
          'Its preface.',
          '',
          '## Weft {Concept}',
          '',
          'A weft is one line of a loom.',
        ),
        '/corpus/post.loom': lines(`A post that speaks of a ${anchor('Weft')}.`),
      },
      '/corpus/book.loom',
    )
    const closing = Option.getOrUndefined(Array.last(pageAt(corpus, 'index')?.blocks ?? []))
    expect(closing?.type === 'prose' ? closing.markdown : '').toBe(
      'Defined in A small book. Used in A post that speaks of a Weft.',
    )
    expect(
      closing?.type === 'prose'
        ? closing.links.map((link) => [link.name, link.targetSlug, link.targetId])
        : [],
    ).toEqual([
      ['A small book', 'corpus/book', 'weft'],
      ['A post that speaks of a Weft', 'corpus/post', ''],
    ])
  })
})

